$article=['date'=>'2026-10-18','slug'=>'dyslexia-signs-in-children','tag'=>'Learning Disabilities','category'=>'Learning Disabilities / Dyslexia','breadcrumb'=>'Dyslexia Signs in Children','meta_title'=>'Dyslexia Signs in Children: Bright but Struggling to Read','description'=>'Recognise dyslexia signs in children whose ability may be hidden by reading difficulty, and learn what teachers, parents and an assessment should examine.','h1'=>'Bright but Struggling: Signs Your Child Might Have Dyslexia, Not a Motivation Problem','dek'=>'A child can reason brilliantly, remember complex ideas and still find accurate, fluent word reading unusually difficult.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Dyslexia signs in children are often mistaken for low effort because the child appears capable in conversation, practical tasks or reasoning. Dyslexia is a specific difficulty involving accurate or fluent word reading and spelling. It is not caused by low intelligence or laziness.</p>
<h2>Why bright children can be missed</h2><p>Strong vocabulary, memory, general knowledge and family support can compensate for early difficulty. A child may memorise books, guess from pictures or use context so effectively that the underlying decoding problem is not obvious until texts become unfamiliar and longer.</p>
<h2>Signs before and during early reading</h2><ul><li>difficulty noticing or manipulating sounds within words;</li><li>slow learning of letter-sound links;</li><li>persistent difficulty sounding out unfamiliar words;</li><li>spelling the same word differently within one task;</li><li>reading that is accurate only when extremely slow;</li><li>avoiding reading aloud or relying heavily on guessing.</li></ul>
<p>No single error establishes dyslexia. Instruction, school attendance, language exposure, vision, hearing and opportunity to practise all matter.</p>
<h2>What it can look like in an older child</h2><p>As workload grows, the child may understand a topic when it is explained but struggle to learn it from text. Homework takes far longer than expected. Written answers contain less knowledge than oral answers. Fatigue, embarrassment and avoidance can then look like a motivation problem.</p>
<h2>Motivation can be an effect, not the cause</h2><p>Repeated effort without success changes how a child approaches reading. They may joke, argue, rush or say they do not care. Before adding consequences, ask whether the task requires a skill that remains effortful.</p><p>This does not mean expectations disappear. It means teaching and support should target the reading process while protecting the child from shame.</p>
<h2>What evidence to gather</h2><p>Compare listening comprehension with independent reading. Keep examples of spelling, note the kinds of words that cause difficulty and ask the teacher about accuracy, fluency and progress after structured instruction. Family history of reading difficulty can also be relevant.</p>
<h2>What an assessment should examine</h2><p>A good evaluation does more than produce an IQ score. It considers developmental and educational history, reading accuracy and fluency, spelling, phonological processing, comprehension and relevant cognitive processes. In multilingual children, language and script exposure must be considered.</p>
<div class="callout-card"><h3>Ability is not the question</h3><p>The useful question is why access to print remains harder than expected despite teaching and opportunity, and which supports allow the child to demonstrate what they know.</p></div>
<h2>What support may follow</h2><p>Results can guide explicit reading instruction, accommodations and school planning. Assessment should explain both needs and strengths in usable language. Explore <a href="/learning-disabilities/dyslexia">dyslexia assessment and support</a> at eMbrace.</p>
